 S~S  SimpleSal Ecosystem README general content guide

    When a repository user is looking at a repository for the first time, they see the root of
    the repository.  In the root are files describing the managed object within the repository,
    each of those files is an information signal from the publisher of the managed object ABOUT
    the managed object.  The software publishing culture uses common signals to communicate.

    The signals are "about" the managed object's data so they are called "metadata":
        1) version control (RELEASE, CHANGELOG);
        2) licensing (LICENSE, COPYING);
        3) README.

    README is the window or lens through which the other files make sense.  At some point back
    in computer history, someone made some notes about it for themselves, and then for others.
    The consumer of the notes will be new to the content, having forgotten, or been given a copy.

    When the software you publish changes, or is adopted for use by others, other files follow.

------------------------------------------  [delete: after copying master to instance]

 S~S  Making modifications to SimpleSal

  A design goal: no modifications to SimpleSal files are needed to modify the behavior or content.

  Modifying an App and SimpleSal at the same time is complex, and also what SimpleSal is made for.

  Nothing in the repository organization blocks having both checked out at the same time.

  The multi-layer design of the workspace model allows a change that breaks EVERYTHING to be undone.

